```python
import re

EMAIL_RE = re.compile(
    r"^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$"
)

def is_valid_email(email: str) -> bool:
    return bool(EMAIL_RE.match(email))
```

Feed it a string, it hands back `True` or `False`. It checks for a name part, an `@`, a domain, and a dot-something at the end like `.com`.

It won't catch every edge case (like some rare but legal addresses) — for stricter checks later, the `email-validator` package is worth a look.
